.Dd October 3, 2026
.Dt BOOTCTL 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm bootctl
.Nd manage boot kernels, menus, and defaults
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Cm status
.Nm
.Cm kernel Ar FILE
.Nm
.Cm config Ar FILE
.Nm
.Cm grub Ar FILE
.Nm
.Cm commit
.Nm
.Cm trial Ar ENTRY
.Nm
.Cm cancel
.Nm
.Cm fallback
.Nm
.Cm env Ar KEY Op Ar VALUE
.Nm
.Fl -target Ar PARTITION COMMAND
.Ar ...
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
command manages the boot partition's kernel slots, menu, and saved and
trial boot entries.
It normally uses
.Pa /dev/bootpart ,
which only root may open.
The kernel selects the boot disk from
.Ql rach2.boot= ,
otherwise from an identity-selected root, otherwise from disk 0.
The
.Fl -target Ar PARTITION
option selects a named boot partition, such as
.Pa /dev/disk0p1 .
The boot partition is partition 1 of an MBR or a GUID table: on a GUID
table, and on an MBR disk converted by
.Ql rach2-install --update ,
it is the EFI system partition, from which UEFI firmware starts GRUB's EFI
image, and the slots, the menu and the trial boot work there as they do on
an MBR disk, since GRUB opens the same menu under either firmware.
The volume must be FAT32 with two FATs of at most 1024 sectors and at most
131070 clusters, which a 64 MiB partition at one sector to a cluster has.
The
.Cm grub
and
.Cm commit
commands do not accept this option because they act on the booted disk.
Each command requires the operand count shown in the synopsis.
.Pp
Boot-partition commands read
.Pa /proc/cmdline .
Installing a kernel requires a
.Ql rach2.slot=
token identifying a candidate, fallback, or recovery boot.
Committing requires
.Ql rach2.slot=candidate
and an environment block whose
.Ql next_entry
is present and empty, the mark a consumed trial leaves.
GRUB saves this marker before loading the selected kernel.
The environment fields can be edited and do not prove that a boot completed
or identify the kernel bytes that ran.
.Pp
The commands are:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm status
Report the installed files, saved default, next boot, and current boot.
Sector extents for the candidate, fallback, environment block, and menu
are reported in this field order:
.Dl bootctl: NAME: first sector N, N sectors in N clusters, N bytes
The names are
.Ql candidate ,
.Ql fallback ,
.Ql grubenv ,
and
.Ql boot.cfg .
A disk built before the menu slot existed reports:
.Dl bootctl: boot.cfg: absent (a disk built before the menu slot existed)
.Pp
The
.Ql saved_entry
and
.Ql next_entry
lines give the value,
.Ql empty ,
or
.Ql absent .
An empty
.Ql next_entry
is reported as
.Ql empty (a trial was consumed) .
.Pp
The
.Ql bootctl: booted:
line reports
.Ql candidate ,
.Ql fallback ,
.Ql recovery, from the recovery partition and neither slot ,
or
.Ql not from a slot (no rach2.slot= on /proc/cmdline) .
.It Cm kernel Ar FILE
Validate the file and the fallback kernel's header prefix.
The file must be a kernel of the architecture
.Nm
was built for: an ELF32 i386 kernel on i686, whose program headers the
Multiboot loader follows, or an ELF64 x86-64 kernel on x86-64, whose
Multiboot header gives the load's addresses through the a.out kludge.
It must also have a multiboot2 header, which GRUB's menu on a GUID table
loads by: its tags must end with an end tag inside the header, a tag GRUB
must honour must be an address, entry, console or framebuffer tag, and an
address tag comes with an entry tag and gives a load judged as the kludge's.
An x86-64 kernel's multiboot2 header must have them.
Both headers must lie in the first 8192 bytes, the bytes the slot checks
read back.
A load given by addresses must name its end: a load to the end of the file
would run to the end of the slot, which pads the file.
Load file ranges must fit the file size.
Load memory ranges must start at or above 1 MiB and have a representable
32-bit exclusive end.
The entry point must lie in a load's file-backed bytes.
The file must carry the read-write disk-policy banner and not the read-only
one: a kernel built read-only refuses every write command, so its mount of a
disk root fails and the machine stops with nothing mounted.
The fallback's header prefix is judged without that banner, which may lie
past the bytes read.
Set
.Ql saved_entry=fallback ,
write the candidate slot, read it back byte for byte, and set
.Ql next_entry=candidate .
Exactly one reboot tries the new kernel.
A power cycle after a crash returns to the fallback.
After installation, the command reports:
.Dl bootctl: installed: N bytes in the candidate slot; the next boot tries it once, and a failure falls back
.Dl bootctl: run `bootctl commit` after the new kernel boots
.It Cm commit
Make the candidate the saved default.
Require
.Ql rach2.slot=candidate
and a present, empty
.Ql next_entry
in the environment block.
Success is reported as:
.Dl bootctl: committed: the candidate is the saved default
.It Cm trial Ar ENTRY
Arm one boot of
.Ql candidate ,
.Ql fallback ,
or
.Ql recovery .
Other entry names are refused.
.It Cm cancel
Remove
.Ql next_entry
so that the next boot uses the saved default.
The key is removed rather than set to the empty value left by a consumed trial.
Success is reported as:
.Dl bootctl: cancelled: the next boot takes the saved default
.It Cm fallback
Copy the candidate into the fallback slot and read it back.
Require
.Ql saved_entry=candidate ,
no nonempty
.Ql next_entry ,
and a fallback file at least as long as the candidate slot.
Invalidate the fallback's first sector, copy and verify the remaining
sectors, then restore and verify the first sector.
A failed payload write or readback leaves an invalid header that stops
a later install.
A failed final write may leave a partial header.
The command then prints:
.Dl bootctl: run `bootctl trial fallback` to boot it once
.It Cm config Ar FILE
Write and verify the fixed menu slot.
GRUB reads this file after defining its stub entries.
The stub defines the required entries before sourcing this file.
The file is unrestricted GRUB script and can change the variables used
to select the default or boot a kernel directly.
An empty file, a file larger than the slot, or a disk without a menu
slot is refused.
.It Cm grub Ar FILE
Replace the i386-pc GRUB core image through
.Pa /dev/grubcore ,
the window over the sectors that hold GRUB's BIOS core.
On an MBR disk the window is the gap after the master boot record,
sectors 1 through 2047, and the kernel publishes it only when no partition
overlaps those sectors.
On a disk with a GUID table it is the BIOS boot partition, sectors 34
through 2047 on a disk this system installs, and the kernel publishes it
only when the table has exactly one such partition, clear of the table's
own sectors.
The command reads the window's first sector and size from the kernel with
.Dv HDIO_GETGEO
and
.Dv BLKGETSIZE64 .
.Pp
The window's first sector is the entry sector, which the boot code in
sector 0 loads, and the rest holds two tails of equal length, each at most
1023 sectors: 1023 at sectors 2 and 1025 on an MBR disk, 1006 at sectors 35
and 1041 in the BIOS boot partition.
The image must be at least 2 sectors and at most one sector longer than a
tail: 1024 sectors on an MBR disk, 1007 in the BIOS boot partition.
It is written to the tail the entry sector does not name, the whole tail
is read back, and only then is the entry sector rewritten to name it.
A failure before that write leaves the old core selected.
No sector outside the window is written, so sector 0, the partition table
and the boot code's pointer to the entry sector stay as the disk was
built.
The command does not check that the image has the modules the disk needs:
.Ql part_gpt
for a GUID table,
.Ql part_msdos
for an MBR.
On success it reports:
.Dl bootctl: installed: N sectors of boot loader in the tail at LBA N; the entry sector at LBA N now points there
.It Cm env Ar KEY Op Ar VALUE
Read or set
.Ql saved_entry
or
.Ql next_entry .
No other keys are accepted.
Without
.Ar VALUE ,
report the key's value or absence.
Values may not contain control characters and must be
.Ql candidate ,
.Ql fallback ,
or
.Ql recovery .
An empty value is refused because an empty
.Ql next_entry
is the marker left by a trial boot.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
Install a kernel from the running system, reboot to try it, then inspect
and commit it:
.Dl bootctl kernel /tmp/kernel
.Dl reboot
.Dl bootctl status && bootctl commit
.Sh DIAGNOSTICS
A kernel file that cannot be opened or read is reported as
.Ql bootctl: cannot open 'FILE'
or
.Ql bootctl: reading 'FILE' failed .
A file too large for any slot is reported as
.Ql bootctl: 'FILE' is larger than any slot ,
and a kernel of the other architecture as
.Ql bootctl: 'FILE' is not an i686 kernel, which this system's programs need ,
or the same with x86-64.
Without a recognized boot slot, installation reports:
.Dl bootctl: this boot did not come from a kernel slot (no rach2.slot= on /proc/cmdline)
A commit from another boot reports:
.Dl bootctl: only a machine running the candidate may commit it (rach2.slot=candidate is not on /proc/cmdline)
.Pp
An unsupported trial entry is reported as
.Ql bootctl: not a menu entry this disk has: 'ENTRY' .
An unsupported environment key is reported as
.Ql bootctl: not a key this tool writes: 'KEY' .
.Pp
Menu refusals include
.Ql bootctl: 'FILE' is empty ,
.Ql bootctl: 'FILE' is N bytes and the menu slot has N ,
and
.Ql bootctl: this disk has no menu slot; it was built before one existed .
An unavailable GRUB window is reported as:
.Dl bootctl: this machine has no boot loader window (no /dev/grubcore)
A core image longer than the window's tails is reported as:
.Dl bootctl: 'FILE' is N sectors and a tail slot of this window holds N after the entry sector: the core image does not fit a tail slot
.Pp
A partition that cannot be read as a boot partition is reported with
its path and the reason, for example that its first sector could not be
read or that the sector has no FAT signature.
For such a refusal with
.Fl -target ,
the command also reports
.Pa /dev/bootpart
as the machine's own boot partition.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr disk 7 ,
.Xr recovery 7 ,
.Xr rach2-install 8
